WEBVTT

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:03.300
Welcome.
Thank you for joining me on this

00:00:03.330 --> 00:00:07.140
exploration of the Silver
Reed LK150 Knitting Machine.

00:00:07.170 --> 00:00:10.600
This is a fun, versatile machine that many

00:00:10.630 --> 00:00:14.540
knitters use, and you may have
been curious about it as well.

00:00:14.570 --> 00:00:16.120
Follow along with me,

00:00:16.150 --> 00:00:21.960
and we'll talk about why it's so popular
and what you can do with this machine.

00:00:22.200 --> 00:00:24.020
Now, before we go any further,

00:00:24.050 --> 00:00:27.060
I want to remind you
that Knit It Now has a Glossary.

00:00:27.080 --> 00:00:30.860
You may hear me use some terms
today that you're not familiar with.

00:00:30.890 --> 00:00:34.760
Go to the Glossary,
it's go to >learn and then down

00:00:34.790 --> 00:00:40.760
to >Glossary, and there's an alphabetical
listing and also a visual listing of terms

00:00:40.790 --> 00:00:46.820
and words that are used specifically with
machine knitting and knitting in general.

00:00:46.840 --> 00:00:50.300
So if you hear something and you go,
What in the world is she talking about?

00:00:50.330 --> 00:00:53.940
Go to the Glossary
and you'll get a definition.

00:00:53.970 --> 00:00:55.940
Why is this machine so popular?

00:00:55.970 --> 00:00:59.100
I believe one of the first
reasons is that it's lightweight.

00:00:59.130 --> 00:01:01.200
It is a plastic bed machine,

00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:03.640
which makes it lightweight,
and you can carry it around easily.

00:01:03.670 --> 00:01:08.050
You can take it up and down
without hurting your back.

00:01:08.080 --> 00:01:10.460
Many knitters will take
it when they travel.

00:01:10.490 --> 00:01:12.570
Maybe take it in your RV.

00:01:12.600 --> 00:01:17.460
You can also put it in maybe a shotgun
case and take it on an airplane.

00:01:17.480 --> 00:01:18.800
I've been known to do that,

00:01:18.830 --> 00:01:23.340
to take it to a hotel room and then
clamp it to the vanity in a bathroom.

00:01:23.370 --> 00:01:26.660
And voila, I've got a knitting station.

00:01:26.690 --> 00:01:29.080
Good lighting and nobody can bother me,

00:01:29.110 --> 00:01:32.620
and I can do some knitting
while I'm on the road.

00:01:32.650 --> 00:01:33.570
It's durable.

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:39.440
It's made with a durable plastic
that can last a long, long time.

00:01:39.920 --> 00:01:42.820
Its needles have little
ball bearings on the top.

00:01:42.850 --> 00:01:44.850
If you notice here in the image,

00:01:44.880 --> 00:01:50.020
the pink there are these little ball
bearing caps on the needle bed,

00:01:50.050 --> 00:01:53.720
on the needle butt,
and it makes it easier to push

00:01:53.750 --> 00:01:57.380
the carriage back and forth,
so it's easy to use.

00:01:57.410 --> 00:02:01.100
Another nice thing about this
machine is it is expandable.

00:02:01.130 --> 00:02:06.740
You can expand it to 200, to 250,
to 300 needles if you want.

00:02:06.760 --> 00:02:08.440
As long as you have a table wide enough

00:02:08.470 --> 00:02:14.320
to support it,
you can knit wider pieces with it.

00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:17.960
Another big reason that this machine is

00:02:17.990 --> 00:02:21.520
popular is it's a great way to get
started in machine knitting.

00:02:21.550 --> 00:02:26.680
It's reasonably priced,
and many knitters start here and then

00:02:26.710 --> 00:02:29.580
quickly realize that they
want a little bit more.

00:02:29.610 --> 00:02:34.520
But for For now, let's have
some fun with the LK150.

00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:39.640
Now, my partner Matt recently asked me,
if somebody already owns a knitting

00:02:39.670 --> 00:02:42.500
machine, why would they
want to buy this one?

00:02:42.530 --> 00:02:44.380
Well, there's a couple of reasons.

00:02:44.410 --> 00:02:47.560
First off, it's a mid gage machine, 6.

00:02:47.590 --> 00:02:53.180
5 millimeters between the needles,
which means that it's going to take...

00:02:53.210 --> 00:02:58.400
It's going to use a wide variety of yarns,
everything from a sock weight all the way

00:02:58.430 --> 00:03:03.300
up to a light worsted weight yarn,
decay, fingering, everything in between.

00:03:03.330 --> 00:03:06.220
So it takes a nice wide range of yarn.

00:03:06.250 --> 00:03:10.320
A lot of knitters start with a
standard gage machine, 4.

00:03:10.350 --> 00:03:11.780
5 millimeters.

00:03:11.810 --> 00:03:14.560
Takes thinner yarn, maybe lace weight,

00:03:14.590 --> 00:03:18.020
even stock weight
and a lot smaller stitches.

00:03:18.050 --> 00:03:24.040
So a lot of knitters like to go to the mid
gage because they can use a wider variety

00:03:24.040 --> 00:03:27.860
of yarns, a lot of the hand-knitting yarns
that you can buy in any yarn store,

00:03:27.890 --> 00:03:32.240
and the stitches are bigger and they're
easy easier to see and manipulate.

00:03:33.360 --> 00:03:35.240
As I mentioned before,

00:03:35.240 --> 00:03:38.380
a lot of knitters like to travel
with their machines, just like me.

00:03:38.410 --> 00:03:39.760
And this one's lightweight.

00:03:39.790 --> 00:03:42.580
You can put it in your motorhome.

00:03:42.610 --> 00:03:47.900
One of the biggest features,
I think, is that it's easy to learn.

00:03:47.930 --> 00:03:52.260
There are really only four things
that you need to know about this machine.

00:03:52.290 --> 00:03:55.020
They're not a whole lot
of buttons and levers.

00:03:55.050 --> 00:04:02.980
The manual is actually thin because it's
very, very simple to learn this machine.

00:04:03.010 --> 00:04:07.480
And another reason that someone
who already has a machine would want this

00:04:07.510 --> 00:04:10.380
LK150 is that you can
quickly use up your stash.

00:04:10.400 --> 00:04:13.360
Or if you're a brand new knitter,
maybe you're a hand knitter and you've got

00:04:13.390 --> 00:04:16.980
this huge stash of yarn
that you want to use up.

00:04:17.010 --> 00:04:20.420
The LK150 is quick and easy to learn.

00:04:20.450 --> 00:04:22.720
Throw some yarn at it and you can make

00:04:22.750 --> 00:04:25.000
blankets and scarves
and hats and whatever.

00:04:25.030 --> 00:04:29.080
Learn to do those really, really quickly.

00:04:32.680 --> 00:04:38.460
As with any knitting machine,
you can knit quickly.

00:04:38.480 --> 00:04:42.240
And if you've had any experience with
knitting machines, just think about this.

00:04:42.270 --> 00:04:47.700
You started out as a hand knitter and you
laboriously knit one stitch at a time.

00:04:47.720 --> 00:04:53.500
But think about knitting 150
stitches with one pass of the carriage.

00:04:53.530 --> 00:04:58.420
It goes so quickly,
and you can get things done.

00:04:58.450 --> 00:05:02.060
Now, can you do absolutely Really
everything on a knitting machine?

00:05:02.080 --> 00:05:04.780
No.
And should you try?

00:05:04.800 --> 00:05:07.060
In my opinion, no.

00:05:07.090 --> 00:05:09.560
My motto has always been,

00:05:09.590 --> 00:05:16.220
do the boring stuff by machine and do
the hard stuff or the fun stuff by hand.

00:05:16.250 --> 00:05:18.740
A really good example of that are these

00:05:18.770 --> 00:05:23.420
round neck, round yoke sweaters
that are so popular right now.

00:05:23.440 --> 00:05:26.860
Can you knit one of those round yoke
sweaters on the knitting machine?

00:05:26.890 --> 00:05:28.460
Well, sure.

00:05:28.480 --> 00:05:29.840
I personally wouldn't.

00:05:29.860 --> 00:05:32.260
Knit, pick up your hand knitting needles,

00:05:32.290 --> 00:05:37.080
knit that round yoke sweater in the round
with all the colors and all the stranded

00:05:37.100 --> 00:05:39.300
knitting and everything
else that you want.

00:05:39.330 --> 00:05:44.100
And then when you've got the hard stuff
done or the fun stuff done,

00:05:44.130 --> 00:05:48.440
pick up those stitches and knit the boring
sleeve, stockinette sleeves and the boring

00:05:48.470 --> 00:05:52.180
stockinette bodies by machine,
and you can be done in no time.

00:05:52.210 --> 00:05:54.560
I mentioned the word versatile before.

00:05:54.590 --> 00:06:00.020
Another advantage of this machine is that,
again, it takes a wide variety of yarns.

00:06:00.040 --> 00:06:03.180
So you might have a lot
of yarns in your stash.

00:06:03.200 --> 00:06:03.700
It's great.

00:06:03.730 --> 00:06:09.040
You can't use them all on a
standard gage machine.

00:06:09.080 --> 00:06:10.540
But on this mid gage,

00:06:10.570 --> 00:06:15.600
you can choose whatever yarn you
want and knit it however you want.

00:06:18.120 --> 00:06:20.120
So I mentioned that there were four things

00:06:20.150 --> 00:06:23.100
that you really need
to know to use this machine.

00:06:23.130 --> 00:06:24.460
There's only four things.

00:06:24.480 --> 00:06:26.720
Now, compared to this pass-up machine

00:06:26.750 --> 00:06:33.060
that I just got, oh, my gosh, there's
a zillion things to learn on that machine.

00:06:33.090 --> 00:06:37.300
On the LK, there's just really
four things that you need to know.

00:06:37.330 --> 00:06:39.820
The first one is the tension dial,

00:06:39.850 --> 00:06:43.640
the relation of this tension dial
and the numbers on the tension dial

00:06:43.670 --> 00:06:46.700
to the stitch size that you're
going to be knitting.

00:06:46.730 --> 00:06:51.720
I had quite a long discussion at one time
with a knitter who insisted that these

00:06:51.750 --> 00:06:56.020
numbers on this tension dial
equalled hand knitting needles.

00:06:56.040 --> 00:07:01.760
Well, number one, if you're thinking hand
knitting needles in millimeters or hand

00:07:01.790 --> 00:07:05.620
knitting needles in US sizes,
there's a difference.

00:07:05.650 --> 00:07:11.060
Number two, I may pick up a size five
needle and knit at a specific gage.

00:07:11.090 --> 00:07:13.040
You may pick up a size five needle

00:07:13.040 --> 00:07:16.260
in the same yarn, and your gage is
going to be completely different.

00:07:16.290 --> 00:07:18.680
And I guarantee you that a size,

00:07:18.710 --> 00:07:24.220
a number five on the LK150 is going
to be even different from that.

00:07:24.250 --> 00:07:29.580
So no, these numbers do not correspond
with hand knitting needle sizes.

00:07:29.600 --> 00:07:32.680
However, However, it might be a good way

00:07:32.710 --> 00:07:36.460
for you to knit your
get-acquainted swatch.

00:07:36.480 --> 00:07:40.820
A get-acquainted swatch is to take a brand
new yarn that you may have purchased,

00:07:40.850 --> 00:07:44.980
put it on the machine,
cast on at a starting tension,

00:07:45.000 --> 00:07:47.740
and then a little bit,
and feel it and see what you think.

00:07:47.760 --> 00:07:51.200
And then maybe change the tension
by a full number and feel it and see what

00:07:51.230 --> 00:07:58.420
you think, and do that up and down
the scale from 2 to 5 or 5 to 7, whatever.

00:07:58.450 --> 00:08:04.940
So if the ball of the yarn that you
purchased suggests a size 5 US needle,

00:08:04.970 --> 00:08:07.860
you might start with a five
on your tension dial.

00:08:07.890 --> 00:08:15.460
It's just a good starting place for where
to start to establish what your gage is.

00:08:15.480 --> 00:08:17.800
So the second thing that you need to know

00:08:17.830 --> 00:08:20.940
and really understand
are the needle positions.

00:08:20.970 --> 00:08:23.520
This is what controls patterns and stitch

00:08:23.540 --> 00:08:26.580
patterning and how your
machine actually works.

00:08:26.600 --> 00:08:28.720
So if you don't remember anything else

00:08:28.750 --> 00:08:33.300
about today, please Please really
pay attention to needle positions.

00:08:33.320 --> 00:08:36.920
There are little number indicators
on the left-hand side of any machine.

00:08:36.940 --> 00:08:38.960
This is not just the LK150.

00:08:38.960 --> 00:08:40.720
Any machine is going to have A, B, C,

00:08:40.750 --> 00:08:45.680
and D, or A, B, D, and E,
or E, and F, or Q, and Z.

00:08:45.700 --> 00:08:47.820
I don't care what the letter is.

00:08:47.850 --> 00:08:52.580
It's the positions that make a difference
as to how the machine operates.

00:08:52.610 --> 00:08:56.840
If your needles, you see the pink needles
at the top right there are all the way

00:08:56.870 --> 00:09:00.000
back, those are in A position
or out-of-work position.

00:09:00.030 --> 00:09:02.480
That means nothing's going to happen when

00:09:02.510 --> 00:09:07.020
you're knitting with
the needles in that position.

00:09:07.050 --> 00:09:09.240
I'll show you what happens underneath

00:09:09.270 --> 00:09:12.740
the machine in just a second,
but stick with me.

00:09:12.770 --> 00:09:14.840
The second position would be your B

00:09:14.870 --> 00:09:17.340
position, which would be
your working position.

00:09:17.370 --> 00:09:21.160
So when you're knitting,
if needles are in this position,

00:09:21.190 --> 00:09:24.980
they're going to go through the track
underneath that carriage and

00:09:25.010 --> 00:09:29.580
be moved into position for them
to pick up yarn and form stitches.

00:09:29.610 --> 00:09:32.860
So working position is forming stitches.

00:09:32.890 --> 00:09:39.100
The C position is middle position,
and this is used when you are paterning.

00:09:39.130 --> 00:09:41.140
And those needles

00:09:41.160 --> 00:09:44.440
that are in that C position are going
to go through a different track underneath

00:09:44.470 --> 00:09:51.100
your carriage and do something else,
depending on how your carriage is set.

00:09:51.130 --> 00:09:54.700
And then D position is all the way out.

00:09:54.730 --> 00:09:59.340
This would be your hold position or
on this machine, your tuck position.

00:09:59.370 --> 00:10:01.900
Needles will not knit.

00:10:01.930 --> 00:10:04.200
So you would use this
for short row shaping.

00:10:04.230 --> 00:10:07.840
You would use this for bridging.

00:10:08.240 --> 00:10:12.020
So these four needle
positions are very important.

00:10:12.050 --> 00:10:15.020
I mentioned the tracks
underneath the carriage.

00:10:15.050 --> 00:10:16.980
As you're putting the needles

00:10:17.000 --> 00:10:19.760
through this carriage,
as you're passing the carriage along,

00:10:19.790 --> 00:10:26.300
those little round ball bearings are
traveling through these red lines.

00:10:26.320 --> 00:10:27.800
And across the very back here,

00:10:27.820 --> 00:10:32.140
you see absolutely nothing is going
to happen on this back bed here.

00:10:32.170 --> 00:10:34.020
So if the needles are in A position,

00:10:34.050 --> 00:10:37.500
they're just going to pass through
and nothing's going to happen.

00:10:37.530 --> 00:10:43.380
If the needles travel through this track,
they're going to be pulled out,

00:10:43.410 --> 00:10:48.360
the latch is opened with magnets,
the yarn will be positioned in the hook

00:10:48.390 --> 00:10:53.100
of the needle, the hook will be
pulled back, and a stitch will form.

00:10:53.130 --> 00:10:57.560
Keep this in mind as you are thinking
about the position of the needles and how

00:10:57.580 --> 00:11:02.400
you're setting your carriage and how
you're creating stitch patterns.

00:11:03.920 --> 00:11:08.080
The third thing that you must know
and understand about this machine,

00:11:08.110 --> 00:11:13.180
and actually any machine,
are the controls for paterning.

00:11:13.210 --> 00:11:15.460
In this case, it's the side levers.

00:11:15.490 --> 00:11:19.540
These side levers are the ones that have
the little dot in a triangle,

00:11:19.570 --> 00:11:22.440
and it just has a little lever
that goes back and forth.

00:11:22.440 --> 00:11:23.080
When

00:11:24.080 --> 00:11:29.460
the lever is in the triangle position,
the needles in working position will pass

00:11:29.490 --> 00:11:33.680
through the channel under the carriage,
and they will knit.

00:11:34.200 --> 00:11:37.800
Set to the circle,
the needles in the working position will

00:11:37.830 --> 00:11:42.720
not pass through the channel,
and they will slip and not knit.

00:11:46.640 --> 00:11:49.360
With the Russell lever set to single line

00:11:49.390 --> 00:11:53.740
like this, the needles in D
position will not knit.

00:11:53.770 --> 00:11:57.980
This is used for partial
knitting or short rows and tuck.

00:11:58.010 --> 00:12:03.060
With the lever set to the double
the needles in D position will be forced

00:12:03.080 --> 00:12:07.360
through the channel under the carriage,
and they will knit.

00:12:11.000 --> 00:12:14.200
You can create almost any stitch pattern

00:12:14.230 --> 00:12:20.380
on this LK150 machine that you can create
on other machines, single bed machines.

00:12:20.410 --> 00:12:22.160
Tuck, slip, ferrile, lace,

00:12:22.190 --> 00:12:26.220
and tarsal plating, weaving,
all of those things can be done.

00:12:26.250 --> 00:12:27.420
How?

00:12:27.450 --> 00:12:29.820
By manually positioning the needles.

00:12:29.850 --> 00:12:32.940
Here Here we go back
to the needle positions again.

00:12:32.970 --> 00:12:39.200
On the LK150, in any manual machine,
when you personally position the needles

00:12:39.230 --> 00:12:43.200
where they need to be,
and you set those levers where they need

00:12:43.230 --> 00:12:47.900
to be, you can create stitch
patterns just like any other machine.

00:12:47.930 --> 00:12:50.340
Now, on any automatic patterning machine,

00:12:50.370 --> 00:12:55.460
your punch card or your electronics are
going to select the needles for you.

00:12:55.490 --> 00:13:01.640
On the LK150, keeping it really simple,
you tell it what needles are going to be

00:13:01.670 --> 00:13:09.560
put into work and what needles are
going to be held or talked or slipped.

00:13:10.040 --> 00:13:13.880
So yes, the LK150 can do almost anything

00:13:13.910 --> 00:13:17.420
that any other machine can do,
but it does have some limitations.

00:13:17.450 --> 00:13:19.300
It is a manual machine.

00:13:19.330 --> 00:13:22.860
You have to select needles yourself.

00:13:22.890 --> 00:13:26.880
Now, you can do hand manipulation,
just like on any other machine.

00:13:26.880 --> 00:13:28.080
You can transfer stitches,

00:13:28.110 --> 00:13:32.300
you can reform stitches to make
garter stitches, for example.

00:13:32.330 --> 00:13:36.260
You can cross cables,
do all those things by hand.

00:13:36.280 --> 00:13:38.000
You need to do all those things by hand

00:13:38.030 --> 00:13:43.100
on any other machine as well, so you're
not missing out with an LK150 there.

00:13:43.130 --> 00:13:48.160
One thing the LK150 never had a ribber,
so you're never going to have a ribber.

00:13:48.160 --> 00:13:49.760
If you don't know what a ribber is,

00:13:49.790 --> 00:13:53.180
you've got needles on your
needle bed going this way.

00:13:53.210 --> 00:13:55.920
If you put a ribber,
it's like adding a second machine.

00:13:55.950 --> 00:14:00.040
You've got needles going this way,
so you've got knit and curl stitches.

00:14:00.070 --> 00:14:02.380
Stitches to do ribbing, for example,

00:14:02.410 --> 00:14:08.140
or any other fancy stitch
that has knit and Pearl stitches.

00:14:08.170 --> 00:14:11.780
Now, some people will
call the LK150 a toy.

00:14:11.810 --> 00:14:14.020
It is not designed for heavy use.

00:14:14.050 --> 00:14:15.980
It is plastic.

00:14:16.010 --> 00:14:17.720
We've got these Bell Bed machines

00:14:17.750 --> 00:14:23.700
that were made in the '60s, maybe even
earlier, and they're still going strong.

00:14:23.730 --> 00:14:26.180
You're not going to wear those babies out.

00:14:26.210 --> 00:14:32.620
You can wear out the LK150, so we
always recommend that you use good yarn.

00:14:32.650 --> 00:14:36.780
Please don't use the hobby
store or a discount store.

00:14:36.810 --> 00:14:42.940
No red heart on the LK150, please,
because you can wear the machine out.

00:14:42.970 --> 00:14:48.140
So you need to be careful that way and use
a yarn that's appropriate for the machine.

00:14:48.170 --> 00:14:53.040
No hand spun, no thick old
lopey hand-spun for your LK150.

00:14:53.070 --> 00:14:56.180
It's only 6.
5 millimeters between those needles.

00:14:56.210 --> 00:14:59.900
So take care of the yarn
that you're using.

00:14:59.930 --> 00:15:04.280
A
lot of knitters will ask for LK150

00:15:04.310 --> 00:15:07.160
knitting patterns,
and I see this quite often online.

00:15:07.190 --> 00:15:10.500
You have any patterns
for an LK150 machine?

00:15:10.530 --> 00:15:12.980
There's no such thing, really.

00:15:13.010 --> 00:15:15.760
It's just, knitting
patterns are just math.

00:15:15.790 --> 00:15:17.340
They're just a blueprint.

00:15:17.370 --> 00:15:20.020
A knitting pattern is mathematical.

00:15:20.050 --> 00:15:21.660
Based on your gage,

00:15:21.690 --> 00:15:26.940
it'll tell you cast on X number
of stitches, knit for X number of rows,

00:15:26.970 --> 00:15:32.700
decrease every row three times, decrease
every other row four times and bind off.

00:15:32.730 --> 00:15:37.980
It's just math, and it's just the same
no matter what machine you're using.

00:15:38.010 --> 00:15:42.340
If you want an LK150 pattern,

00:15:42.370 --> 00:15:45.980
you're going to have to look
long and hard for it.

00:15:46.000 --> 00:15:47.360
And what's going to be the difference

00:15:47.390 --> 00:15:53.300
between that and a pattern
for a Brother 970?

00:15:53.330 --> 00:15:54.760
Really no different.

00:15:54.760 --> 00:15:56.560
I'm going to cast on so many stitches,

00:15:56.580 --> 00:15:59.860
I'm going to knit so many rows,
and I'm going to do the decreases.

00:15:59.890 --> 00:16:01.740
So So there's really no difference.

00:16:01.770 --> 00:16:05.440
And if you need a knitting pattern to tell

00:16:05.470 --> 00:16:10.260
you which Russell lever to push or
which side lever to push, shame on you.

00:16:10.290 --> 00:16:12.520
You need to learn what those Russell

00:16:12.550 --> 00:16:18.300
levers and what those side levers
do on your own so you understand.

00:16:18.330 --> 00:16:23.220
Please understand what your machine can
do, and don't rely on someone to tell you,

00:16:23.250 --> 00:16:26.940
push the A button or push
the C button or whatever.

00:16:26.960 --> 00:16:29.280
It's the same thing with this
pass-up that I'm working on.

00:16:29.310 --> 00:16:31.900
It's got AX and BX and CX and DX.

00:16:31.930 --> 00:16:33.840
What does that mean?
Tell me what it means.

00:16:33.870 --> 00:16:35.960
I've got to understand what's going

00:16:35.990 --> 00:16:43.620
on with my machine and not depend on a
pattern to tell me which lever to push.

00:16:43.650 --> 00:16:46.200
Figure it out on your own,
you'll be a lot happier.

00:16:46.200 --> 00:16:47.600
And you can use hand knitting patterns.

00:16:47.630 --> 00:16:53.120
You can use any pattern that give
you decent mathematical instructions.

00:16:53.360 --> 00:16:58.220
Now, the LK150 has some accessories.

00:16:58.250 --> 00:17:03.860
We're knitters, and we always like to buy
the latest toys to go with our machines.

00:17:03.890 --> 00:17:06.160
And there are some toys that you can

00:17:06.190 --> 00:17:11.010
purchase and that you can
accumulate for your LK150.

00:17:11.040 --> 00:17:13.700
The first one is a second carriage,

00:17:13.730 --> 00:17:17.810
and a lot of knitters invest
in a second carriage for their machine.

00:17:17.840 --> 00:17:21.860
It makes it a lot easier if you're
knitting multi-colors.

00:17:21.890 --> 00:17:24.770
For example, let's see you're
knitting in black and white.

00:17:24.800 --> 00:17:26.800
So you have black on this side

00:17:26.830 --> 00:17:29.980
in a carriage, and you have white
in the carriage on this side.

00:17:30.010 --> 00:17:32.220
You'll knit two rows in the black,

00:17:32.250 --> 00:17:34.940
two rows in the white,
two rows in the black,

00:17:34.970 --> 00:17:39.810
two rows in the white, without having to
remove the yarn and rethread the carriage.

00:17:39.840 --> 00:17:43.180
Really comes in handy if you're
doing a lot of color work.

00:17:43.210 --> 00:17:47.010
So a second carriage is
a really good investment.

00:17:47.040 --> 00:17:50.810
Once upon a time, there were
ferrile carriages for your machine.

00:17:50.840 --> 00:17:57.140
A ferrile carriage will allow you
to put black and white in the same...

00:17:57.170 --> 00:18:01.140
A
ferrile carriage It will allow you to put

00:18:01.170 --> 00:18:07.180
black and white in the same color
in the same carriage at the same time.

00:18:07.210 --> 00:18:10.080
So you can position your needles by hand.

00:18:10.080 --> 00:18:11.360
You're still going to have to pull some

00:18:11.390 --> 00:18:16.280
needles out, but push your carriage across
one time, and it'll knit black stitches,

00:18:16.280 --> 00:18:20.050
white stitches, black stitches, white
stitches with one pass of the carriage.

00:18:20.080 --> 00:18:22.120
Now, I go through all that.

00:18:22.150 --> 00:18:26.380
Fair-isle carriages are as
rare to find as hens teeth.

00:18:26.410 --> 00:18:29.050
Don't you love that expression?

00:18:29.080 --> 00:18:31.660
They are very rare and hard to find.

00:18:31.690 --> 00:18:34.290
They aren't making them anymore, sadly.

00:18:34.320 --> 00:18:36.720
So to do fair oil on an LK150,

00:18:36.750 --> 00:18:40.570
you need to do two passes
of the carriage for each row.

00:18:40.600 --> 00:18:42.280
You're doing black and white again.

00:18:42.280 --> 00:18:44.010
Thread your carriage up with black,

00:18:44.040 --> 00:18:47.010
pull some needles out,
knit the black stitches,

00:18:47.040 --> 00:18:53.290
free pass, remove the black,
free pass back, put in the white,

00:18:53.320 --> 00:18:57.050
pull the other needles out,
knit across, knit the white stitches.

00:18:57.080 --> 00:19:00.500
So you've knit one row with two
passes of the carriage.

00:19:00.530 --> 00:19:02.810
So ferrile can be a little tedious,

00:19:02.840 --> 00:19:07.680
but it's doable, and it's so much fun,
and it's so beautiful when it's done.

00:19:07.710 --> 00:19:10.500
You don't need a ferrile carriage.

00:19:10.530 --> 00:19:17.530
An intarsia car, a carriage that is
available is the intarja carriage.

00:19:17.560 --> 00:19:24.720
Now, again, it's the same idea of being
able to knit multiple colors across a row.

00:19:24.720 --> 00:19:26.880
Intarja.
Think about intarja as blocks of color.

00:19:26.910 --> 00:19:31.770
So you've got a black
block and a white block.

00:19:31.800 --> 00:19:35.860
You would knit that in two
passes of the carriage again.

00:19:35.890 --> 00:19:37.040
You could...

00:19:37.070 --> 00:19:47.570
So think of Intarja as color blocks,
a black block and a white block.

00:19:47.600 --> 00:19:48.960
With an intarsia carriage,

00:19:48.990 --> 00:19:53.220
you could do both black and white
in one pass of the carriage.

00:19:53.250 --> 00:19:54.940
You would thread up the black,

00:19:54.970 --> 00:19:59.920
for example, and then you would
loop or you would

00:20:00.840 --> 00:20:07.000
thread up the black, for example,
and then you would lay the white yarn.

00:20:07.030 --> 00:20:12.880
Thread up the black, for example,
in the carriage, and then lay the white

00:20:12.910 --> 00:20:16.010
yarn across the needles
that you want to knit in white.

00:20:16.040 --> 00:20:18.900
Pass the intarsia carriage across once,

00:20:18.930 --> 00:20:23.500
and both black and white
stitches will knit.

00:20:23.530 --> 00:20:26.080
Very handy for doing that type of work,

00:20:26.110 --> 00:20:29.380
an Intarsia Carriage,
and those are available.

00:20:29.410 --> 00:20:33.700
Now in In this image here, you can
see something called a needle beetle.

00:20:33.730 --> 00:20:38.770
This is a really thrifted, really nifty...

00:20:38.800 --> 00:20:42.660
In this image here, you can see
something called a needle beetle.

00:20:42.690 --> 00:20:48.940
It's a really nifty little accessory
that is, I think of it as a memorizer.

00:20:48.970 --> 00:20:55.770
The way it works is you select the first
eight needles, you do it in groups of...

00:20:55.800 --> 00:20:59.680
I like to think of it as the memorizer.

00:20:59.710 --> 00:21:05.530
You choose your needles in groups
of eight, so eight stitch multiples.

00:21:05.560 --> 00:21:10.330
So let's say I choose every other
needle and pull it out manually.

00:21:10.360 --> 00:21:13.240
I then run my needle Beatle across those

00:21:13.270 --> 00:21:19.640
eight needles and it memorizes every other
needle and then continues to select every

00:21:19.670 --> 00:21:24.180
other needle in that sequence
all the way across the bed.

00:21:24.210 --> 00:21:29.010
So again, if you were if you were
knitting every fourth needle out of...

00:21:29.040 --> 00:21:32.700
If you took every a fourth needle and put
it into position,

00:21:32.730 --> 00:21:37.980
you could memorize
the first eight and then go across the bed

00:21:38.010 --> 00:21:41.460
and it repeats that sequence
across the bed.

00:21:41.490 --> 00:21:44.040
Needle Beatle is something
that is also for sale.

00:21:44.070 --> 00:21:46.280
They're very, very handy if you like to do

00:21:46.310 --> 00:21:48.800
pattern work and you don't
have to think as much.

00:21:48.830 --> 00:21:51.740
You just remember to position your first

00:21:51.770 --> 00:21:56.720
eight multiples, and then it
repeats it across the row.

00:21:57.040 --> 00:21:59.500
Another accessory is a garter bar.

00:21:59.530 --> 00:22:02.570
A garter Garter bar allows you to

00:22:02.600 --> 00:22:07.220
remove stitches from the machine
and put them back on again very easily.

00:22:07.250 --> 00:22:11.330
You could do all 150 if you
had a garter bar wide enough.

00:22:11.360 --> 00:22:14.480
Now, garter bars for this
machine at the 6.

00:22:14.510 --> 00:22:17.900
5 millimeter are also very,
very hard to find.

00:22:17.930 --> 00:22:22.220
I won't say anything about hens teeth,
but they are very difficult to find.

00:22:22.250 --> 00:22:24.560
Look for a garter bar that's a 6.

00:22:24.590 --> 00:22:30.660
5 millimeter, and you'd be able
to remove the stitches from...

00:22:30.680 --> 00:22:32.240
You'd be able to remove the stitches

00:22:32.270 --> 00:22:35.500
from the bed,
take the knitting off the bed,

00:22:35.530 --> 00:22:40.380
turn everything around, put the stitches
back on the bed, and knit another row.

00:22:40.410 --> 00:22:44.330
By turning it around, you'll now
have pearl stitches facing you.

00:22:44.360 --> 00:22:46.420
Take the knitting off the bed,

00:22:46.450 --> 00:22:51.380
turn it around, and knit it again so you
can alternate knit & Pearl stitches

00:22:51.410 --> 00:22:55.540
and create some garter stitch,
which would be really tedious,

00:22:55.570 --> 00:22:59.540
but for short little bits of garter
stitch, you could do that.

00:22:59.570 --> 00:23:03.330
You can I also use a garter
bar for holding stitches.

00:23:03.360 --> 00:23:07.020
So they're very handy,
even though they're hard to find.

00:23:07.050 --> 00:23:10.220
Another accessory is a patterning device.

00:23:10.250 --> 00:23:15.860
The one that's available for this
machine is a KR10 or KR11 11.

00:23:15.890 --> 00:23:18.200
These, again, are no longer being made.

00:23:18.230 --> 00:23:22.540
They're something you'd have to look for,
but they're not so difficult to find.

00:23:22.570 --> 00:23:27.050
The charting device, and I believe
I have a little diagram here.

00:23:27.080 --> 00:23:29.220
Here's a charting device.

00:23:29.240 --> 00:23:31.290
If you think about it, this little device,

00:23:31.320 --> 00:23:36.090
as you push your carriage back and forth,
it increments your row counter.

00:23:36.120 --> 00:23:41.180
So on this machine, you attach
the charting device to your row counter.

00:23:41.210 --> 00:23:47.090
And as you knit,
the row counter, the CROCounter advances

00:23:47.120 --> 00:23:52.940
the paper drawing of your garment,
and you can follow the lines

00:23:52.970 --> 00:23:57.260
and do your increases and decreases
without thinking about numbers.

00:23:57.290 --> 00:24:00.360
So instead of saying,
decrease one stitch every two rows 10

00:24:00.390 --> 00:24:06.020
times, you just decrease as
the little line passes the indicator.

00:24:06.050 --> 00:24:08.900
Charting devices are a great tool.

00:24:08.930 --> 00:24:13.810
If you can find one, if you're making
garments, they're really, really helpful.

00:24:13.840 --> 00:24:17.720
And again, they're something
It's not as easy to find.

00:24:19.000 --> 00:24:23.960
Third accessory,
call it an accessory, is software.

00:24:23.990 --> 00:24:26.620
Designinit is a piece as such as...

00:24:26.650 --> 00:24:30.240
So here's a picture
of the charting device.

00:24:32.760 --> 00:24:37.660
The LK150 and Designinit.

00:24:37.690 --> 00:24:42.180
Designinit is software
that allows you to do a lot.

00:24:42.210 --> 00:24:48.260
It allows you to create garment shapes so
you can create your own knitting patterns.

00:24:48.290 --> 00:24:52.420
It allows you to visually
design stitch patterns.

00:24:52.450 --> 00:24:58.020
On the screen makes it very
easy to design stitch patterns.

00:24:58.050 --> 00:25:02.200
Most people think of designing it
with electronic knitting machines because

00:25:02.230 --> 00:25:06.290
one of the features of designing it is
that you can design stitch patterns

00:25:06.320 --> 00:25:09.860
and then download them to an
electronic knitting machine.

00:25:09.890 --> 00:25:11.840
So the electronic knitting machine then

00:25:11.870 --> 00:25:15.900
will select your needles for you based
on what you designed in and design a knit.

00:25:15.930 --> 00:25:18.600
Obviously, we're not going
to do that with our LK150.

00:25:18.630 --> 00:25:21.780
There's no electronics in our
little plastic machine.

00:25:21.810 --> 00:25:26.120
However, you can create stitch patterns
and stitch pattern diagrams that you can

00:25:26.150 --> 00:25:30.220
follow to manually pull
and select needles.

00:25:30.250 --> 00:25:34.940
Another thing that you can do with design
a knit is create your garment shapes.

00:25:34.970 --> 00:25:39.540
So you want to make yourself a sweater
that is set in sleeves with a V- neck,

00:25:39.570 --> 00:25:45.420
and it's got a fitted waist, and it's
A-line, and it's got a curved hem.

00:25:45.450 --> 00:25:47.800
No problem.
Do it in design a knit,

00:25:47.830 --> 00:25:51.200
and then design a knit,
you can use the design in its interactive

00:25:51.230 --> 00:25:57.260
knitting as a charting device and follow
the lines and do your shaping.

00:25:57.290 --> 00:26:04.560
You need a cable, and There is a little
magnet here that acts as

00:26:05.000 --> 00:26:10.220
a tripper for the charting device, the
interactive knitting part of Designinit.

00:26:10.250 --> 00:26:12.000
I'll link to the video and some

00:26:12.030 --> 00:26:16.290
of the other things I've talked about
here in the description of this video.

00:26:16.320 --> 00:26:19.940
So you can create stitch pattern diagrams,
you can create your garments,

00:26:19.970 --> 00:26:27.540
you can use the interactive knitting,
all with design a knit and your LK150.

00:26:27.570 --> 00:26:29.960
So just because you have a plastic bed

00:26:29.960 --> 00:26:32.860
machine, machine doesn't mean
you're limited in what you can do.

00:26:32.880 --> 00:26:36.320
As you can see, you can do all the stitch
patterns, you can use software,

00:26:36.350 --> 00:26:40.980
you can do all the things you
can do with any other machine.

00:26:41.010 --> 00:26:46.180
Here's an example of the interactive
knitting screen in design it.

00:26:46.210 --> 00:26:47.800
So you would just follow along.

00:26:47.830 --> 00:26:51.360
It tells you exactly which
needles to do your shaping.

00:26:51.390 --> 00:26:53.540
We're shaping the top of a sleeve here,

00:26:53.570 --> 00:26:56.680
and it tells you exactly
where to do your shaping.

00:26:56.710 --> 00:26:58.050
It's saying decrease.

00:26:58.080 --> 00:26:59.320
And she actually has a little voice.

00:26:59.350 --> 00:27:04.280
She'll say, Decrease one,
left, decrease one right.

00:27:04.680 --> 00:27:09.840
Keeps you on track, and you don't have
to think about numbers and keeping track.

00:27:10.800 --> 00:27:13.040
This whole time, I keep talking about how

00:27:13.070 --> 00:27:17.660
easy it is to learn the LK150 fifty,
and it is easy to learn.

00:27:17.690 --> 00:27:21.220
One of the challenges is
to take it step by step.

00:27:21.240 --> 00:27:23.120
I can't tell you how many knitters say

00:27:23.150 --> 00:27:28.810
to me, I just got my machine last week and
I want to knit my son-in-law a sweater.

00:27:28.840 --> 00:27:31.020
Okay, Good for you.

00:27:31.050 --> 00:27:32.500
Can you cast on?

00:27:32.530 --> 00:27:33.860
How's your knitting?

00:27:33.880 --> 00:27:35.780
Do you want to put a stitch pattern in?

00:27:35.810 --> 00:27:38.180
Have you increased and decreased?

00:27:38.210 --> 00:27:40.940
Do you know how to put stitches and hold?

00:27:40.970 --> 00:27:42.360
What about binding off?

00:27:42.390 --> 00:27:47.960
I mean, there are so many things to learn
that but not necessarily machine-specific.

00:27:48.080 --> 00:27:56.900
So we've created a series of courses
that are machine-specific for your LK150.

00:27:56.930 --> 00:28:03.020
And we do tell you, push this lever
